WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.130
- I am so excited to
introduce our next guest.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.070
I am so thrilled that she is here.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.970
She is a philanthropist,

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.500
a UN Messenger of Peace,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.580
mother,

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.090
award winning actor who
is currently right now,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.727
in one of the top Netflix
films of all times,

00:00:19.727 --> 00:00:21.280
"The Old Guard."

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.279
Ladies and gentlemen,
it's Charlize Theron.

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:27.839
(audience applauding)

00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:28.672
- Hi.

00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:29.900
- Hi.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.233
(screaming)

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.130
I'm probably gonna have to redo the intro

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.310
because of my lifelong lisp.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.530
I'm like Netflix (mumbles)

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.710
It's the best thing on Netflix.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.230
- You better not touch that.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.680
That is what we love about you.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.010
So do not touch that.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.570
- Whenever I see someone parody me

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.790
which I'm always like so excited that I...

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.400
Anyone would even know who
I am, let alone parody me.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.435
It's always like the
craziest face of the lisp.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:01.850
(laughing)
And I'm just like dying,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:02.683
laughing.

00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:03.516
I'm like, "It's true."

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.953
- Oh my God the way
you just did that face,

00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:09.350
that's amazing.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.000
- If someone was gonna do you

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.600
they would just get
taller, and more beautiful,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.660
and more worldly, and
philanthropic, and smart.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.493
It's true.

00:01:19.493 --> 00:01:20.326
- That's not true.

00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:22.310
Once I did "Monster"
everything I did after that

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.143
there were like,

00:01:23.143 --> 00:01:25.980
"Oh, another ugly movie,
another ugly movie."

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.470
And SNL did a skit

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.600
where literally it was just
like me walking around,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.275
trying to look like a baby's
arm growing out of my head.

00:01:34.275 --> 00:01:35.730
(laughing)
Just trying to be

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.020
as uncomfortable looking as possible.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.737
And I was like, "Guys, I
did one movie like that.

00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:42.670
"Stop it."

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.520
- We have a friend in common.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.840
And you even mentioned
this in your Oscar speech.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.210
This man, Steve Warren.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.043
- Yes.

00:01:49.043 --> 00:01:52.320
I remember I said my lawyer, Steve Warren.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.070
I heard just like
snickering in the audience.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.560
Like, "Wait, you can't think your lawyer."

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.273
And I was like, "He's my friend."

00:01:57.273 --> 00:01:58.450
(chuckles)

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:59.450
He's amazing.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.370
And I don't have to tell you this.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:02.660
He's changed my life.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.800
I think all of the beautiful things

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.280
that I have in my life today,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.010
Steve somehow has something to do with it.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.580
- We actually have
something in common too,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.980
which is I've heard you're on
a break from relationships.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.500
- Yeah.
- And I'm so in love

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.333
with my children.

00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:25.610
I have this love in my
life that's so impactful.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.670
And I've spent my life
trying to figure out men

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:28.630
for 45 years.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:29.707
I'm like, "I'm tired, I'm bored.

00:02:29.707 --> 00:02:31.619
"It's all about the kids."

00:02:31.619 --> 00:02:32.780
- Yeah.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.800
I completely know what you're saying.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.090
And it is strange for people

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.670
to kind of wrap their heads around it.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.370
I've been on a few dates,

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.329
but I haven't dated anybody
for over five years.

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:45.162
(exclaims)

00:02:45.162 --> 00:02:45.995
- Me too.

00:02:46.848 --> 00:02:49.130
- Yeah, so we have a lot in common.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.617
So, I'm open when
friends of mine are like,

00:02:51.617 --> 00:02:53.380
"You should go on a date,
you should meet this guy."

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.010
Like I'm always open.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.890
But it is like,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.290
I feel like I'm in a place in my life

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.986
where you gotta come with a lot of game.

00:03:03.986 --> 00:03:06.010
Not the kind of game that we think of,

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.540
the kind of game that's
like my life is really good,

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.560
so you better be able to
bring that and maybe better.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.660
'Cause I just won't to
accept anything less.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.190
And my life with my children,

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.340
and with my incredible adopted
family that I have around me,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:24.650
I don't long for that much.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.220
I don't really long...

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.860
I can honestly say this on my life,

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.340
I don't feel lonely.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.279
Once I had my children,

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:36.700
it's not that it replaces something,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.200
or that it makes you less
interested in something else,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.560
like I'm still...

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.190
I was just two seconds
ago talking about a dude

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.690
that I thought was really hot.

00:03:45.776 --> 00:03:47.930
I'm still firing in all cylinders,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.790
I just...

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.520
I think your priorities are in a place

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.500
that is of high demand, right?

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.010
'Cause it's a lot of work to be a parent.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.150
And then part of that
is at the end of the day

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.067
I get in bed and I go,

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:06.450
"My God, I wouldn't want this
day to be anything different.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.310
- Yup.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.330
I always say I would want
someone to be an addition

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.630
if not the equation and
never a subtraction.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:15.463
- That's...

00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:16.296
You said it.

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:19.730
I did the poor man's line of that.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:20.563
Thank you for...

00:04:20.563 --> 00:04:21.396
I'm gonna take that.

00:04:21.396 --> 00:04:22.780
I'm stealing that just so you know.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:23.850
- It's for both of us

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.820
since we're both on such a similar path,

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.390
and Jackson is eight?

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:29.260
- Yes.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.663
- And August is five?

00:04:31.663 --> 00:04:32.496
- Yes

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:38.430
- I have Olive who's eight this
month and Frankie who's six.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.360
- Wow, we're so close.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:41.570
- I know.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:46.230
I never realized our kids
were that aligned in ages.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.063
- Yes.

00:04:47.063 --> 00:04:48.190
Super, super close.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:49.770
I had an idea we were close,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.320
but I didn't know we were that close.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.960
- Oh, well gosh, darn the pandemic.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.677
I would be like, "How are
we getting our kids together

00:04:57.677 --> 00:04:59.300
"when things ease up?"

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.493
I would love nothing more.

00:05:01.493 --> 00:05:03.850
The more--
- Yes, the same year.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:04.820
- Oh please.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.660
And my kids, it's so hard
for kids with the adjustments

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.010
and God bless all of them
for being as adaptable

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.230
and sane as some of them are.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.100
I just...

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:23.540
It makes me marvel at their
capacity and adaptability.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.840
How are your kids right now?

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.950
- My mind is blown by that as well.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.100
I have to tell you,

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.950
there's a part of all of this
that's so devastating, right?

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:37.870
Like we are really truly
living in a whole new world.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.810
And when I look at my children

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.540
and how they're just
adapting to it so well.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:44.373
This...

00:05:44.373 --> 00:05:45.206
Just the idea,

00:05:45.206 --> 00:05:46.510
like the other day I
walked out of the house

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:47.650
and I had to walk back into the house

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:48.800
'cause I forgot my mask.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.200
And I realized my children
don't forget their masks.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.320
They literally always
have their masks on them.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.780
I'm like, I'm the one that always like,

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.428
I walk out, I gotta go back in and get...

00:05:57.428 --> 00:05:59.260
They have just embraced

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.928
that this is what their
world is right now.

00:06:00.928 --> 00:06:04.590
And I have to say even the homeschooling,

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.340
when we went into that first lockdown,

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.930
it was really, really hard
because they're kids, right?

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.130
And so they just don't fully comprehend

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.167
but by now they figured out,

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:15.770
"Okay, this is what it is."

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.620
And it's like, it's really different.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.330
They've kind of embraced this now.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.720
And they still can share
that they miss their friends,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.610
and that they miss going to school.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.760
But at the same time they're
just not dwelling on it,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.170
which is just so incredible.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.513
It's like, they're just moving on with it.

00:06:30.513 --> 00:06:33.680
(bright upbeat music)

